  "concept": {
    "movement": "A heavy amber moth holds its long abdomen centered while four broad eye-spotted wings beat steadily. Forewings lead and hindwings follow gently, with tucked six-legged suspension and restrained feeler overlap. Slow flight is deliberate; fast flight makes the stroke quicker without bouncing the torso.",
    "attack": "It opens its forelegs and wings in a broad warning draw, then drives forward and down in a heavy head-led swoop, the front pair reaching ahead. A strong restoring beat returns it to the hovering guard.",
    "death": "A failing wingbeat lets the centered body sink and tip forward. The actual thorax and abdomen settle, the four eye-spotted wings relax low, all six legs release around the torso, and both feelers droop into a supported, motionless finish."
